These four procedures are the core steps in Oracle’s online table redefinition process. They’re used in sequence to restructure a table (for example, converting it into a partitioned table, moving it to a new tablespace, or changing its column definitions) while keeping the table online for queries and DML.

🔧 When to Use Each Procedure
DBMS_REDEFINITION.START_REDEF_TABLE

When: At the beginning of the redefinition process.

Purpose: Starts copying data from the original table into the interim table (the one with the new structure).

Use case: You’ve created an interim table with the desired structure and now want to begin the redefinition.

DBMS_REDEFINITION.COPY_TABLE_DEPENDENTS

When: After starting redefinition, before finishing.

Purpose: Copies dependent objects (indexes, triggers, constraints, grants, privileges) from the original table to the interim table.

Use case: Ensures the new table has all the same supporting objects as the old one.

DBMS_REDEFINITION.SYNC_INTERIM_TABLE

When: During the redefinition process, especially if the original table is still being updated by users.

Purpose: Synchronizes changes (DML operations like INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) made to the original table into the interim table.

Use case: Keeps the interim table up to date with the original while redefinition is ongoing.

DBMS_REDEFINITION.FINISH_REDEF_TABLE

When: At the end of the redefinition process.

Purpose: Completes the redefinition, swaps the interim table in place of the original, and makes it the active table.

Use case: Final step — after this, the interim table becomes the new definition of the original table.

📌 Typical Workflow
Create interim table with new structure.

Call START_REDEF_TABLE.

Call COPY_TABLE_DEPENDENTS.

Optionally call SYNC_INTERIM_TABLE multiple times if DML is ongoing.

Call FINISH_REDEF_TABLE to complete the process.

👉 In short:

Start → begin redefinition.

Copy dependents → bring over indexes/triggers/constraints.

Sync → keep interim table current with ongoing changes.

Finish → finalize and swap in the new structure.

*/

👉 In short: these four procedures are used whenever you want to redefine an existing table online — whether that means partitioning it, changing its structure, or moving it. They are not only for creating a new partitioned base table, but for any structural transformation of an existing table without downtime.

👉 In short: structural transformation means changing the table’s definition (partitioning, storage, columns, etc.), but Oracle allows DML during the process. That’s the whole advantage of online redefinition — you don’t need downtime.

✅ Step-by-Step Correct Flow
1️⃣    Start Redefinition
BEGIN
  DBMS_REDEFINITION.start_redef_table(
    uname      => HR,
    orig_table => EMP,
    int_table  => EMP_PART
  );
END;
/

2️⃣     ⭐ Copy ALL Dependent Objects (CRITICAL)
BEGIN
  DBMS_REDEFINITION.copy_table_dependents(
    uname            => HR,
    orig_table       => EMP,
    int_table        => EMP_PART,
    copy_indexes     => DBMS_REDEFINITION.CONS_ORIG_PARAMS,
    copy_triggers    => TRUE,
    copy_constraints => TRUE,
    copy_privileges  => TRUE
  );
END;
/

📌 This copies:

✔ Primary / Foreign / Unique constraints
✔ Triggers
✔ Indexes
✔ Grants

3️⃣      (Optional but Recommended) Sync Interim Table

If DML is happening:

BEGIN
  DBMS_REDEFINITION.sync_interim_table(
    uname      => HR,
    orig_table => EMP,
    int_table  => EMP_PART
  );
END;
/

4️⃣     Finish Redefinition (Swap Happens Here)
BEGIN
  DBMS_REDEFINITION.finish_redef_table(
    uname      => HR,
    orig_table => EMP,
    int_table  => EMP_PART
  );
END;
/


👉 At this point:

EMP = partitioned table

All constraints & triggers are back on EMP

Interim table is dropped automatically

